---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/volnlabs/actryn';
const commit = 'a6b0004130ac';
const evidence = (path: string) => `${repo}/blob/${commit}/${path}`;
---
<Base title="Actryn" description="Actryn keeps operation identity, authority and recovery explicit across lost replies and restarts. The first step toward evidence-backed robot releases.">
  <div class="min">
    <main id="main-content">
      <div class="pill quiet"><span class="dot"></span>v0.0.1 software-only developer preview</div>
      <h1>The reply never arrived. Did the robot move<span class="accent">?</span></h1>
      <p class="lead"><strong>Our first step toward evidence-backed robot releases.</strong> Actryn focuses on one part of the problem: keeping operation identity, authority and recovery explicit across lost replies and restarts.</p>

      <figure class="film">
        <video controls preload="metadata" playsinline poster="/video/actryn-explainer.jpg">
          <source src="/video/actryn-explainer.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track kind="captions" src="/video/actryn-explainer.vtt" srclang="en" label="English" default>
        </video>
        <figcaption>The idea in a minute and a half, then the real v0.0.1 software doing it. Nothing physical moves; the controller is simulated.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="run">The run</h2>
      <figure class="film">
        <video controls preload="none" playsinline poster="/video/actryn-v0.0.1-run.jpg">
          <source src="/video/actryn-v0.0.1-run.mp4" type="video/mp4">
        </video>
        <figcaption>The full recorded take on the signed v0.0.1 release: a dropped controller reply stays <code>OutcomeUnknown</code>, new work is refused until recovery, the next operation completes and both are exported. One continuous take in two QEMU virtual machines; waits sped up.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>The v0.0.1 operator workflow, run end to end by the maintainer on the exact release binaries. The final signed bundles were then verified with a fresh installation. Every step leaves evidence that is exported at the end.</p>
      <ol class="sequence">
        <li><h3>Install and diagnose</h3><p>Verify and activate the Server and Robot roles. Services start inhibited; installing and pairing grant no authority.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Pair and authorize</h3><p>An isolated agent proposes one typed <code>wheel.index_step.v1</code> skill. A local operator reviews it and grants finite, scoped authority.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Two operations, in order</h3><p>The offline preview agent proposes two operations. The second becomes eligible only after the first is reconciled.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Cancel</h3><p>A submitted operation is cancelled through its retained handle, not by issuing a replacement.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Restart and recover</h3><p>Services restart and an explicit recover begins. Retained state is reconciled against the controller before any new work is allowed.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Export</h3><p>Server, robot and kernel evidence is exported and bound to the signed release.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Evidence</h2>
      <div class="wide-stack">
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Release</div>
          <p>Actryn v0.0.1, qualified at commit <a href={`${repo}/commit/${commit}`}><code>{commit}</code></a> on 2026-10-19. Server and Robot manifests are signed with a published Ed25519 key.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Environment</div>
          <p>Server: Debian 13.7 amd64, Linux 6.12.111. Robot: Debian 13.7 arm64 QEMU guest, Linux 6.12.111, with the Actryn kernel authority module and a deterministic, non-actuating fixture-wire-v2 controller.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Exercised behavior</div>
          <p>Crash and restart recovery with uncertain outcomes preserved, cancellation before the first reply, stale and replayed request rejection, blocking new work until reconciliation, and keeping unresolved work charged.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Records</div>
          <p><a href={evidence('docs/development/v0.0.1-release-qualification.json')}>Qualification receipt</a> · <a href={evidence('docs/development/signed-distribution-verification.json')}>signed-distribution verification</a> · <a href={evidence('docs/development/final-distribution-archive-restore.json')}>archive restore</a> · <a href={evidence('docs/development/v0.0.1-signed-metadata/README.md')}>public key and manifests</a>. Check them yourself with <code>python3 -B ci/signed_distribution.py --require-signed --require-qualified</code>.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Scope</div>
          <ul class="rule-list">
            <li><strong>Software reproduction · done</strong><p>Full workflow run by the maintainer on the exact release binaries. 188 qualification receipts verified, and the signed distribution verified separately.</p></li>
            <li><strong>Independent replication · done</strong><p>A second operator has installed and run the release.</p></li>
            <li><strong>Physical testing · not yet</strong><p>The shipped target does not actuate hardware. A controller acknowledgement here is not evidence of physical motion.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <h2>Why</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p>If a command reply is lost, resending may repeat a physical action. Assuming success may hide incomplete work.</p>
        <p><strong>Actryn keeps the outcome unknown until the original operation is reconciled.</strong> New work remains blocked, and the system does not automatically resubmit the action.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <ol class="sequence">
        <li><h3>Propose</h3><p>An isolated agent proposes one typed skill with bounded parameters, not generated robot-local code.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Authorize</h3><p>A local operator grants finite, scoped authority. Installing or pairing grants none.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Enforce</h3><p>A Linux authority gate enforces grants, lifetime, revocation and stale-input rejection.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Reconcile</h3><p>Controller state and evidence resolve uncertain outcomes before new work becomes eligible.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What exists today</h2>
      <ul class="rule-list">
        <li><strong>Native CLI and Python SDK</strong><p>One operation identity is retained from proposal to outcome across crashes and restarts.</p></li>
        <li><strong>Server and robot services</strong><p>Signed role bundles, cancellation, replay rejection, crash recovery and evidence export.</p></li>
        <li><strong>Linux kernel authority module</strong><p>Run under QEMU against a deterministic, non-actuating controller fixture.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why Actryn, alongside AxiomOS</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p>Design partners wanted a full driver path without replacing their existing stack. Actryn is the narrower product for selected Linux environments: it keeps existing drivers and applies lessons from AxiomOS at the controller boundary.</p>
        <p>AxiomOS continues as the broader programmable-kernel research program. Actryn is not a rename or a replacement for it.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Start with one release</h2>
      <p>Bring one inspection workflow and one behavior change you need to evaluate. We’ll scope the failure cases and evidence for that release decision, starting from your current stack.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/contact">Discuss a release assessment</a>
        <a href={repo} class="secondary">View Actryn on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
